`,e.jsxs(i.li,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Allow Override Enrollment"})," (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"enable_replaceable_enrollment"}),"): allows an already-enrolled device to enroll again through ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"/simpleenroll"}),". When active, the previous certificate is revoked with the reason ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"superseded"})," and the identity slot is updated. When inactive, a second ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"simpleenroll"})," attempt is rejected with ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"forbiddenNewEnrollment"}),". This option must be enabled with care in production environments, since it lets you replace a device's identity without going through the re-enrollment flow."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.li,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Verify CSR Signature"})," (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"verify_csr_signature"}),"): controls whether the server validates the CSR signature against the public key declared in it (PKCS#10 proof of possession). When inactive, a requester could submit a CSR with a public key it does not control and obtain a certificate bound to it. For IoT fleets on open networks it must be enabled."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.li,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Authentication Mode"}),": defines how the DMS validates the device identity during enrollment. It can require an mTLS certificate (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"CLIENT_CERTIFICATE"}),"), delegate authorization to a webhook (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK"}),"), combine both controls (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_AND_EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK"}),") or accept unauthenticated requests (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"NO_AUTH"}),"). The latter must be limited to isolated labs or provisioning networks."]}),`
